## Architecture rules
- User data (history, reminders, settings, custom apps) lives in device localStorage via `src/lib/store.ts`; no login/backend yet — keeps NB private and simple until sync is requested.
- AI command planning runs only in the `planCommand` server function (`src/lib/planner.functions.ts`) with a strict schema; the client executes steps and must log truthful statuses (`needs_user` when the result can't be verified).
- Sensitive steps (messages, calls) always go through the confirmation dialog; the user presses the final send/call.
- App registry is data (`src/lib/apps.ts` + user custom apps); add apps there, never hardcode in UI.
- Native Android code lives in `android-kit/` and is untested until built in Android Studio; never describe it as working.
- In the Android app, always-on listening runs in `VoiceForegroundService` (native SpeechRecognizer loop) and delivers commands via the NbActions "command" event; the web mic loop is skipped when native — two listeners would fight over the mic.
- Planner, modes, workflows, preferences and safety guards are pure functions in `src/lib/agent.ts` (tested in `src/test/agent.test.ts`); pages only call them — keeps safety rules testable.
- Workflows execute through the assistant's `runPlan`, so mode rules, confirmations and the 10-minute duplicate-send block apply to every path.
- Voice input uses the browser recognizer first and falls back to NB cloud listening (`src/lib/cloud-listen.ts` → `/api/public/transcribe`) when the phone's speech service fails — Android app wrappers often break the browser recognizer.
- Spoken contact names resolve to numbers only through NB Contacts (`resolveContact` in `src/lib/agent.ts`); never guess a number.
- Debug APKs are built only by `.github/workflows/android-debug-apk.yml`, which wraps the published site in a Capacitor shell created outside the repo and applies `android-kit/` via `android-kit/scripts/apply-kit.py` — keeps the web app free of native build files.

