# NB Android Kit — UNTESTED

These files have **not been compiled or tested**. They are a starting point for turning the NB web app into a native Android app with Capacitor.

## No computer? Build on GitHub
1. In Lovable connect GitHub (Plus → GitHub) so the project is pushed to a repo.
2. On github.com open the repo → **Actions** → **Android debug APK** → **Run workflow**.
3. When it turns green (about 10 minutes), open the run → **Artifacts** → download `nb-debug-apk`, unzip, install the `.apk` on the phone (allow "install unknown apps").
If it turns red, copy the error from the failed step and paste it to NB's builder to fix.
The debug APK is unsigned for the Play Store; use it only for testing.

## With Android Studio

## 1. Create the Android project
```bash
# in the exported project folder (GitHub → clone)
npm i @capacitor/core @capacitor/cli @capacitor/android
npx cap init "NB Assistant" com.example.nb   # replace com.example.nb with your real application ID
npm run build
npx cap add android
npx cap sync android
```
Set `webDir` in `capacitor.config.ts` to the folder produced by the build. Because NB uses server functions for the AI planner, the simplest setup is to point Capacitor at your published URL:
```ts
server: { url: "https://YOUR-PUBLISHED-URL", cleartext: false }
```

## 2. Add the native files
Copy `kotlin/*.kt` into `android/app/src/main/java/com/example/nb/` (fix the package line).
Merge `AndroidManifest.snippet.xml` into `android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml`.
Copy `res/xml/nb_accessibility.xml` into `android/app/src/main/res/xml/`.
Register the plugin in `MainActivity.kt`:
```kotlin
class MainActivity : BridgeActivity() {
  override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
    registerPlugin(AppLauncherPlugin::class.java)
    registerPlugin(NbActionsPlugin::class.java)
    super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
  }
}
```

## 3. Use from the web app
```ts
import { registerPlugin, Capacitor } from "@capacitor/core";
const AppLauncher = registerPlugin<{
  listApps(): Promise<{ apps: { name: string; pkg: string }[] }>;
  launch(o: { pkg: string }): Promise<{ launched: boolean }>;
  openAction(o: { action: string }): Promise<{ launched: boolean }>;
  accessibilityEnabled(): Promise<{ enabled: boolean }>;
  openAccessibilitySettings(): Promise<void>;
}>("AppLauncher");
if (Capacitor.isNativePlatform()) await AppLauncher.launch({ pkg: "com.whatsapp" });
```
Then, in `AssistantProvider.tsx`, call `AppLauncher.launch` for `open_app` steps when running natively, and log `success` only if `launched` is true.

## 4. Build
- Debug APK: Android Studio → Build → Build APK(s). Or `cd android && ./gradlew assembleDebug`.
- Release AAB: create a keystore (`keytool -genkey -v -keystore nb.jks -alias nb -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000`), configure signing in `android/app/build.gradle`, then `./gradlew bundleRelease`.

## 5. Play Store checklist
- [ ] Real application ID, app name, icon (512×512) and feature graphic
- [ ] Privacy Policy URL → `https://YOUR-PUBLISHED-URL/privacy`
- [ ] Data safety form: command text sent to AI provider; history stored on device; no data sold
- [ ] `QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES` declaration (core purpose: launcher/assistant) — Google may reject; fallback is the `<queries>` list in the manifest
- [ ] Accessibility API declaration form + in-app prominent disclosure + video demo
- [ ] Foreground service type `microphone` declaration and justification
- [ ] Screenshots: dashboard, voice, app launcher, reminders, settings (phone + tablet)
- [ ] Test: permission denied paths, cancel, emergency stop, unsupported app, no internet
- [ ] Internal testing track before production

## NbActions plugin (UNTESTED)
`setAlarm({hour,minute,label})`, `openCamera({front,video})`, `call({number,confirmed})`, `whatsappChat({number,text})`,
`playStoreSearch({query})`, `mapsSearch({query})`, `answerCall()`, `declineCall()`,
and Accessibility actions: `tap({label})`, `type({text})`, `scroll({dir})`, `back()`, `readScreen()`.
All return `{ ok, reason? }` — log `success` only when `ok` is true.
Example flow "WhatsApp e Rahim ke video call dao": `AppLauncher.launch(com.whatsapp)` → `tap("Search")` → `type("Rahim")` → `tap("Rahim")` → confirm in NB → `tap("Video call")`.
Like/comment on Facebook, delete in Google Photos, reading messages: same tap/type/readScreen steps; labels differ per app version and language, so each must be tested and NB must ask before any send/delete/post.

## Limits
- Apps cannot install other apps silently — Play Store opens, the user taps Install.
- Strangers' secure/bank apps and FLAG_SECURE screens hide content from Accessibility.
- Android may kill background services; wake word needs a visible notification.
- Banking/payment apps often block Accessibility automation — NB must report failure.
- iOS: no control of other apps. Use Siri Shortcuts / App Intents only.

## Background "NB" listening (UNTESTED until tried on a real phone)
`VoiceForegroundService.kt` listens with Android's own speech recognizer (on-device when the phone has it)
inside a foreground service with a visible "NB is listening" notification and a Stop button.
- Turn **Always listening On** while NB is open; it then keeps listening when you open other apps or turn the screen off.
- Say "NB" once; for 5 minutes after, commands work without repeating the name.
- If Android blocks NB from opening itself, a "NB heard: …" notification appears — tap it to run the command.
- For screen-off listening: Phone Settings → Apps → NB → Battery → Unrestricted. Some phones (Xiaomi, Oppo, Vivo) also need "Autostart" on.
- Limits: it stops if the phone is switched off, if Android kills the service, or when the user taps Stop. Bangla offline recognition needs the Bangla offline speech pack in Google settings.

